#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079420} :activity/id #uuid "5f643ade-7715-5788-91bb-d00dfcf9badd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943 :topic/id #uuid "cdbe026a-f2a4-5c0a-8b9f-a7d7b8060500" :topic/math-academy-id 625} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078276 :question/id #uuid "82814727-3ad9-5e62-a491-086484491d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19695af-0879-5a00-b400-1c4a490f7bfa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519589}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"} :step/id #uuid "e52ecac1-55d5-5dc3-a7ce-c1a112c5ca79" :step/math-academy-id 11988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079615 :tutorial/content "In general, the limit of a function $f(x)$ at a point only exists if *both* the left-sided and right-sided limits *exist* and are the *same*.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)$ for the function whose graph is shown below. To do this, we must first compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/d9/d99cb9753d63d0812245159b6727e30408eb6cd89d01ecb2f4090f65925527f1.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, $y$ approaches $0$. However, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, $y$ approaches $1$. Therefore, we have the following left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Left-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 0 \\\\ Right-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBoth of these limits exist. However, they are *not* the same. Consequently, the overall limit at $x=2$ does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cb3c51e-4afc-5b0f-9559-908488362a25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a826f64-60af-5170-b888-9d2ab6f1a053" :step/math-academy-id 275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079421}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078508 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078371 :question/id #uuid "190af2d8-9441-5445-980b-de5908512f4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552519}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Interior Point"} :step/id #uuid "9edf9846-edef-5bf5-82af-f48490ca2717" :step/math-academy-id 276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078509 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078375 :question/id #uuid "a16e55c8-e740-5dc9-8e5d-809b1ab88f10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11b0cbea-c29d-586f-989d-49c717d58fdd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519516}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Endpoint"} :step/id #uuid "428ef65d-b5b3-5155-832b-8961d7b2d2fe" :step/math-academy-id 279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079260}}] :activity/title "Finding the Existence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078651}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519643} :activity/id #uuid "ce4873bc-0d3b-5f2c-a4a8-7ecb745b9327" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943 :topic/id #uuid "cdbe026a-f2a4-5c0a-8b9f-a7d7b8060500" :topic/math-academy-id 625} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a4015c-7e0e-59a8-af52-660a728632ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519070} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc06de8-5221-52fa-920c-03c18a05eb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519088} {:answer/id #uuid "476e139f-e45f-5859-b5d8-144735e49388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519105} {:answer/id #uuid "77c0f144-34b3-52df-ac81-d4928b60dca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519179} {:answer/id #uuid "89743f02-1d9f-5884-b7b0-ec422e07ca6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519213} :answer-field/id #uuid "9725b865-33c5-5170-8ad4-3ac184e816c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc22d572-0b94-5462-9d85-2c1d61a16284" :question/math-academy-id "q-34999" :question/problem "![](images/39/39c2c86e1fca1dbcf80435237ba31fc4aed9ed8213578b4c5f3971408da59f2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "72d4130c-fc01-5da4-a860-034259704a88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519666}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c19089e-e70c-5d3e-89a1-8b1f43d5dfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} {:answer/id #uuid "5373c9c4-344c-5fdd-ab6a-8f94ae077c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519120} {:answer/id #uuid "78bc99e5-2323-56ec-9a3c-1a503b64e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519181} {:answer/id #uuid "8d69e6d5-8e64-51d5-9437-2837aa08c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519223} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b7b55b-e405-57e3-9cfb-bb0f23fba298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} :answer-field/id #uuid "482e304e-d433-57bd-9c20-e198c527d454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "069bdd21-6b0e-5746-9f9a-48c390ea491e" :question/math-academy-id "q-67539" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96bbf527-f90f-58a7-9dde-fd51c996bbe9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63a73ea-9815-5c7f-80c0-444c5b37376c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076557} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c90e00-5224-516b-81a4-8321e3f2f8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076558} {:answer/id #uuid "20efce61-3023-5a01-b4d5-10268f69d6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076559} {:answer/id #uuid "26be1b29-5328-5c08-aa6e-7aaaaf75c21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076560} {:answer/id #uuid "4a335476-04ef-5f30-85b4-bca18e903569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076561}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186076559} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fad43c5-0056-5e55-96e3-0ec6739ca78a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91103606-4eee-5423-99c0-f8331e850732" :question/math-academy-id "q-47596" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."} :step/id #uuid "d31b4e41-aaa1-5ef0-a230-367e59083067" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519651}}] :activity/title "Finding the Existence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518988}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125504} :activity/id #uuid "f93f830f-ca1a-5ca2-a1ee-45fe0506d2e7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259 :topic/id #uuid "3a3e1a52-75c9-5495-b430-05a58e4d5dde" :topic/math-academy-id 1873} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124467 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124271 :question/id #uuid "f0e213c5-8a7a-51a3-995f-394270a22bd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3559"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cfbdc8f-6c4f-5411-b6f5-b7f0f046ac4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545871}] :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for a Bounded Function"} :step/id #uuid "722ddd1c-5e61-5fe1-9200-a3d2c15284fb" :step/math-academy-id 655038 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125744 :tutorial/content "Not every function $f(x)$ levels off to approach an asymptote as $x$ approaches infinity.\n\nInstead, a function $f(x)$ might get bigger and bigger, increasing without bound, as shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/99/99d75e971e3ac33723bd6a012806af08ae69a5677a7a44553569bdf6706a81ac.png)\n\nIn this case, we say that the limit of the function is infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) = \\infty\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c3614f64-54f5-51dd-8908-af9d99f7da31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2584 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits"} :step/id #uuid "8f7c3cbf-6b88-5be0-b80b-aa8a91f57ff6" :step/math-academy-id 655042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f754170-0622-5c95-a522-eab185c0e2d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 152 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a7a7874-637c-5af9-9935-68417a30cf66" :step/math-academy-id 655037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124579 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124193 :question/id #uuid "a5050a89-0b53-5499-8a78-9d5d7c5a78c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea4038bf-34f0-58cc-a7b6-6a4c1890570f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552441}] :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for an Unbounded Function"} :step/id #uuid "f069f004-01b3-5bd3-845a-6e1c33680f54" :step/math-academy-id 655043}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity from Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124686}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116237} :activity/id #uuid "62f93d8a-2957-5060-b885-5c042e54a174" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050304 :topic/id #uuid "3e73d954-af0d-5100-9b54-139fe502bf1c" :topic/math-academy-id 1716} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116431 :tutorial/content "For any power function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nwe can compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n$ by substituting $x=a$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n = a^n \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2$, we can substitute $x=3$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\,\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nand checking the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/69/69cea7ab1cfb0c0a2201dd857b12eafa9ecf0cf4824abb5d4ca0234e40f3af5f.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^-} x^2 = 9$\n- Right-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^+} x^2 = 9$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $9$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 9 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b82f637a-fa0f-55fc-9a89-f30d492729d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 163 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a8e0c650-d9fa-55bd-a9e6-ff4b7eda7326" :step/math-academy-id 311 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114945 :question/id #uuid "f1a8976c-86e9-5532-b796-03f399fbe58b" :question/math-academy-id "e-19"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7cb6cf1-a195-5723-a663-984bbec3f980" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115806}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Limit Using Direct Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "1a0eb767-0e3a-5cf7-93ee-f032391bd092" :step/math-academy-id 312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116432 :tutorial/content "For any constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=c\n$$\n\nthe limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c$ will match the value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c=c \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1$ is just the value of the constant, $1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a2c17e59dca9161db435fc4b3edf78a22fed7e6ee94219ac29b85627116791ac.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} 1 = 1$\n- Right-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} 1 = 1$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $1$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} 1 = 1 \\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6e18025-cf2d-597a-b7ce-d3fd4d993e5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 166 :tutorial/title "The Limit of a Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "4aaaf85f-2c6b-57a2-9a90-2871c735fa20" :step/math-academy-id 316 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115319 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114955 :question/id #uuid "679639c9-7f28-5f32-afa9-a6dea7e98518" :question/math-academy-id "e-21"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a712927e-48fa-54db-b49c-37a748b4e697" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115800}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "0532f19c-995b-57e2-8d07-89903b7797e4" :step/math-academy-id 317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116433 :tutorial/content "The **constant rule for limits** states that if $c$ is a constant, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}c\\cdot f(x) = c\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=c\\cdot L\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}5x^3$, we just factor the constant $5$ out of the limit and then substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}{\\color{blue}5}x^3 &= {\\color{blue}5} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}x^3 \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}5}\\cdot 2^3 \\\\[5pt] &= 40 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,1} cf(x) = 10}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a real constant. Let's find the value of $c$.\n\n![](images/de/dea6bb963e83928f463ac2b6d09775b0c11ac0ca7c8e3e567c4b51da154c86f9.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we get that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\, 1} f(x)} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)cf(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot 5 &= 10 \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68d19cb3-bb5e-5580-b6c1-50b2aa41a668" :tutorial/math-academy-id 168 :tutorial/title "The Constant Rule for Limits"} :step/id #uuid "c9022dc2-a37c-5b55-8a93-4484e86b585e" :step/math-academy-id 319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115320 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114964 :question/id #uuid "c74237c5-04a7-5e54-8fad-0bfae67cb44a" :question/math-academy-id "e-22"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "843eeda7-fef6-5efe-bcb3-56a569e31fb8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115810}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Rule to Compute the Limit of a Function Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b7fdcc46-3b68-5503-a9e4-5af8140d614e" :step/math-academy-id 320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115326 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114965 :question/id #uuid "9a7e6c72-3850-5b80-b62f-fab23dac2d7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-23"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b94d5612-763f-59d8-9a80-7f6e2f18fa6c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552447}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Rule to Compute a Limit Algebraically"} :step/id #uuid "c57e1ccb-49e2-5531-85dc-aab8af003723" :step/math-academy-id 322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116242}}] :activity/title "Limits of Power Functions, and the Constant Rule for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115465}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069889} :activity/id #uuid "b49354da-478a-59ab-9dea-86e9f0a3ee81" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454 :topic/id #uuid "4a48339a-3719-56ed-a805-f01876009733" :topic/math-academy-id 472} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069933 :tutorial/content "We already know that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a.$ However, there are really two ways that $x$ can approach $a$ -- from the left side or from the right side -- and sometimes, it matters which direction we choose.\n\nFor example, consider the function below.\n\n- As $x \\to -1$ from the left, we have $f(x) \\to -4,$ but\n\n- as $x \\to -1$ from the right, we have $f(x) \\to -3.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7a08cf136b1fdb33973c4f94300b08d18ca89fae8c2f9016155d05e21f4d610.png)\n\nTo be more precise about the direction in which $x$ approaches $a$, we use superscripts. The minus sign $(-)$ means \"from the left\" and the plus sign $(+)$ means \"from the right.\"\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a^-} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a$ *from the left.*\n\n- **Right-sided limit:** $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, a^+} f(x)$ represents the limit of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $a$ *from the right.*\n\nFor the function graphed above, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1^{-}}f(x)=-4\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1^{+}}f(x)=-3 \\,.\n$$\n\nAt the same time, if we consider the point $x=-5,$ we note that the left-sided limit at this point does not exist because the function to the left of $x=-5$ is not defined. If the limit **does not exist**, we usually denote it using the abbreviation $\\textrm{DNE}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-5^{-}}f(x)=\\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"minus\" superscript $(-)$ indicates \"left\" because the left direction is the negative direction on the number line, and the \"plus\" superscript $(+)$ indicates \"right\" because the right direction is the positive direction on the number line." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae1f7cef-eccc-5929-b2af-84b9331df54b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 142 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7d18a65a-31eb-5f80-91de-27c84b295301" :step/math-academy-id 597762 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068546 :question/id #uuid "dbad1aa8-5786-524e-9add-5295231dfd83" :question/math-academy-id "e-3557"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552464}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"} :step/id #uuid "5706d92d-9891-5a0b-8a15-e8594da1476e" :step/math-academy-id 597763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068649 :question/id #uuid "b788cb59-f017-5ebe-9f67-f746517de260" :question/math-academy-id "e-772"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a14c94-c26b-5a3b-bbbb-6f122b5de9de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530542}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at Endpoints"} :step/id #uuid "e45b81ee-ef96-5d67-a130-149eec2cc643" :step/math-academy-id 597767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069892}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069892 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069957 :tutorial/content "If two limits *do not exist*, then we *cannot* say that they are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nSo, if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x) = \\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) = \\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)  \\neq  \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aec5f028-60ef-51ac-9c1d-504eda0168ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2582 :tutorial/title "Comparing Limits that Do Not Exist"} :step/id #uuid "b53a4234-38b7-5f9e-8348-80425a9e5580" :step/math-academy-id 597770 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069893}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068635 :question/id #uuid "24c5fa9b-f91e-5088-9647-4923784c5917" :question/math-academy-id "e-7"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530547}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"} :step/id #uuid "0d3e6999-71d0-5da2-a390-e4a4a55b712a" :step/math-academy-id 597771}] :activity/title "The Left and Right-Sided Limits of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068996}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567} :activity/id #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574 :topic/id #uuid "54ee2cc0-54a0-5d60-a3d0-0b11995e4133" :topic/math-academy-id 462} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fb32bea-3d77-5134-936c-bfa9f0381fdb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2583 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3561bec4-880a-5815-bc18-84c3d62a3191" :step/math-academy-id 12020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513512} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549055}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "1c9dde49-f9f9-5036-8e84-b7e1ced3796b" :step/math-academy-id 12021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514621} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552521}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"} :step/id #uuid "74cd2bd0-c1c2-5429-adda-d13aa18601d2" :step/math-academy-id 12022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514550} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329857}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"} :step/id #uuid "64c70852-38a7-5f5e-ade8-36856f8eef67" :step/math-academy-id 12023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54ca4bef-1b8e-5315-85d4-0667910b332c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2640 :tutorial/title "Continuity at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "c5269250-b2f9-525d-bfaa-9f0e6033687b" :step/math-academy-id 12404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568}}] :activity/title "Determining Continuity from Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068989}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125558} :activity/id #uuid "5be1aba9-8d05-5cc5-8d16-2faeb26e75c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653 :topic/id #uuid "5cd64b32-8cbc-5b0f-b4b4-7b7dad877ee2" :topic/math-academy-id 1914} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124468 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124272 :question/id #uuid "4cbd707a-c3a7-5f68-803f-15941e2fd4b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dad9bdc2-a243-5437-8904-9906c2d9a90e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050304}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235417}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "2c0eea00-4c79-58bd-a141-08872cc00730" :step/math-academy-id 12000 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125718 :tutorial/content "The **sum rule** states that the limit of the sum of two functions equals the sum of the limits (provided that those limits exist).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → a)(f(x) + g(x)) &= lim_(x → a)f(x) + lim_(x → a)g(x) \\\\\n&= L + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,-3} \\left(5x^2+2x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can first apply the sum rule, followed by the constant rule, and compute the limits of the power functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 3)(5x^{2} + 2x) &= lim_(x → - 3)5x^{2} + lim_(x → - 3)2x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x^{2} + 2 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-3)^{2} + 2 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&= 45 - 6 \\\\\n&= 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is a shortcut: in general, to find the limit of a polynomial $f(x)$ at some point $x=c$, all we need to do is evaluate the polynomial at $c$.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\n**Warning!** If one of $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)$ or $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)$ exists while the other does not exist, then $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\Bigl(f(x)+g(x) \\Bigr)$ does not exist either." :tutorial/id #uuid "6a3580c6-229d-54ff-b09f-3bc257eadf2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "de7a6cde-8613-5323-be0d-287774012b07" :step/math-academy-id 324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124238 :question/id #uuid "d1efe60f-bf85-56a4-a711-778a4c2f4003" :question/math-academy-id "e-25"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552439}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute A Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "f4817a4a-39ad-58fc-80ec-554cf0c34b53" :step/math-academy-id 330}] :activity/title "The Sum Rule for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124713}]}
